############################
### Install Instructions ###
############################

### Information ###
The Avade software is an IRC Services software specifically developed to
work with the bahamut IRCd and MariaDB/MySQL database in the background.

These installation instructions will only describe the process of installing
Avade IRC Services and not anything else. 


### The short way ###

As the user that will run services (not root):

    git clone https://github.com/Pintuzoft/Avade.git
    cd Avade
    ./install.sh

install.sh checks that the machine has Java 17, puts everything in ~/avade/,
and starts services. The first time there is no services.conf, so it asks a
few questions (your network, the hub, the database) and writes it for you:

- it tries the database login while you answer, and when that does not work
  it shows the lines to run as the root user of the database
- it shows the lines the hub needs in its ircd.conf, with your names and
  password filled in (also saved in ~/avade/hub-setup.txt)
- the secret salt and the passwords you do not type are made for you

Mail to users is the same: "./avade.sh mailer start" asks for your mail server
the first time, sends a test mail and writes mailer.conf (see 11.).

To upgrade, get the new version and run ./install.sh again. It offers to take
a backup of the database first, keeps services.conf, adds the commands the new
version brought to its access lists, and restarts services.

What you need before you start: a bahamut hub you can change the ircd.conf of,
and MariaDB (or MySQL) with its root login.

The rest of this file describes each step, for when you want to do it by hand
or want to know what happens.


### Install & Update ###


1. Dependencies

Java 17 or newer. Avade comes built (dist/), so the Java runtime is enough:

    Fedora, RHEL, CentOS:   sudo dnf install java-17-openjdk-headless
    Debian, Ubuntu:         sudo apt-get install openjdk-17-jre-headless

To build it yourself you need the JDK (java-17-openjdk-devel, openjdk-17-jdk).
ant is used when it is there, without it ./make.sh builds with javac.


2. Download

Goto and download the latest release from:
https://github.com/Pintuzoft/Avade/releases
or clone the repository as in the short way above.


3. Install

tar -zxvf Avade-<version>.tar.gz
cd Avade-<version>
./install.sh

By hand instead: "./make.sh" builds and "./make.sh install" puts everything in
~/avade/.


4. Setup Database ( MariaDB / MySQL )

The setup tries the login and shows these lines with your names when they are
needed. As the root user of the database:

create database if not exists avade;
create user if not exists 'services'@'localhost' identified by 'CHOOSE-A-PASSWORD';
grant all privileges on avade.* to 'services'@'localhost';


5. Services Configuration

"./avade.sh start" writes services.conf for you the first time, by asking what
is needed. "./avade.sh setup" does only that. The same command, with a
services.conf that is already there, adds the commands a new version brought.

By hand instead: under "~/avade/" you will find the template.conf file. Copy
it to services.conf and set the values. reference.conf explains every setting.

Dont forget to set network name and change the salt to something completely 
random. After setting the salt value please save it in a secure place perhaps in
keepass or similar software. Avade uses the salt to encrypt and decrypt the email
addresses stored in the database, so if you loose the salt value you will loose
the email addresses. Passwords are stored as one way hashes that nobody can read
back; the salt is only needed once for them, when a database from before
1.2609-8 is upgraded.

Set the master nick correctly. This should be the nick you are using on the
server.


6. Starting Services

cd ~/avade
./avade.sh start

start tells whether services linked to the hub, and when they did not, what
is in the way (the hub does not answer, the hub closed the link and why, or
the database can not be reached). Services keep trying by themselves.

Other actions: stop, restart, status, setup, check and log. The output of
services ends up in ~/avade/avade.out (the five latest are kept).

To bring services back after a reboot or a crash, add this to the crontab of
the user running services (crontab -e):

*/5 * * * * $HOME/avade/avade.sh check

The first time you start services it will look at the services configuration 
and try login to the database. Once it does this it will look at the database
and if its empty it will try create the tables it requires. If the database is
running an older version of the database services will try to update it to the
same version as itself automatically.

Once the proper version of the database is available the master nick will be
created (if not already existing) and give the nickname master access.


7. Stopping Services

Use "./avade.sh stop" or the /RootServ STOP command. Both set services into a
mode where it tries to work off all pending database work it can find before
it exits. This means any pending nick or chan registration etc, or services
logs that needs to be stored.

A normal kill does the same thing. Avoid "kill -9" as that will potentially
mean loss of data.


8. Updating Services

Get the new version as described in step 2 and run ./install.sh in it. It
offers to take a backup of the database (to ~/avade/backup/), installs the
new version, adds new commands to the access lists of services.conf, and
restarts services.

By hand instead:
- Take a backup of the database!
- Then start the new version of Avade

Once a new release of Avade has come available you can may download it as 
described in step 2. And then install it as described in step 3. For updating
the database check step 9. Make sure the services.conf is correct by identifying 
new possible settings from template.conf and if any important new settings is 
missing you will see them in the services.log. If you cant start services and
it gives you an error regarding the configuration then consult the reference.conf
and the template.conf and add any missing settings.


9. Database Changes

There will most likely always be database changes between releases, however
unlike other IRC services softwares Avade will always try keep them up2date.
This means that every time Avade is started it checks the config and connect to
the database and it will automatically try apply any database changes it can 
find in order to keep the database correct for that version of Avade.

This way of handling the database changes is ofcourse dangerous but if you avoid
altering data in the database it should be fine. This also means that you 
essentially doesnt have to put much thought into the database during updates. 
All you need to remember is to take a backup of the database before starting
the new version of Avade IRC Services.


10. Host-masking (optional)

Avade can hide the real host of users from other users, together with the
avade_uhm module for bahamut that comes with Avade (the bahamut-module
directory). bahamut itself is not changed.

c-83-233-12-7.bredband.telia.com  ->  mynet-0cabd86f.bredband.telia.com
192.0.2.44                        ->  6c13e277.8057c675.1b690abc.ip

IRC operators still see the real host. How the masks are made is described in
bahamut-module/README.md.

Steps 10.1 and 10.2 are done on every server of the network, the rest once.

Needed on every server:
- bahamut 2.2.0 or newer, check with /VERSION
- the bahamut source that the running ircd was built from, with ./configure
  run in it
- gcc and the OpenSSL headers

CentOS:
yum install gcc
yum install openssl-devel


10.1 Build the module (every server)

cd Avade-<version>/bahamut-module
./build.sh /path/to/bahamut-source /path/to/ircd

The first path is the bahamut source. The second is the directory of the
running ircd, where its ircd.conf is. The module is put in
/path/to/ircd/modules/avade_uhm.so.

The source must be of the same bahamut version as the ircd that runs. If the
machine has several, this shows the version of a source directory:

cd /path/to/bahamut-source
grep -E "define (MAJOR|MINOR|PATCH) " include/patchlevel.h

When bahamut is upgraded the module has to be built again from the new source.


10.2 Load the module (every server)

Add this to ircd.conf, so the module is loaded every time the ircd starts:

modules { path modules; autoload avade_uhm; };

The line is only used when the ircd starts. A /REHASH does not load the module.
To load it without restarting the ircd, as a server admin (oper access A)
connected to that server:

/MODULE LOAD avade_uhm
/MODULE LIST

The list should show:

Module: avade_uhm    Version: 1.0
  - User host-masking that Avade IRC Services can follow

Until services have sent the salt (10.3) the module masks with a random one.
No real host is shown, but the masks are not the ones services expect.


10.3 The salt

The masks are made with a secret salt. Anyone who has it can work out which
mask belongs to which ip, so treat it like a database password.

It is set in one place, the services.conf of Avade. Nothing is put on a server
by hand: Avade sends the salt to the servers, and each server keeps it in the
file avade_uhm.salt next to its ircd.conf.

Make a salt:

cd ~/avade
./avade.sh gensalt

"openssl rand -hex 24" works as well. Put it in services.conf:

uhmsalt: <the salt>
uhmprefix: mynet

- uhmsalt is 16 to 128 letters and digits, nothing else
- uhmsalt must not be the same as secretsalt, services do not start if it is
- uhmprefix is the first part of masked host names, 1 to 16 letters and digits

Restart services:

./avade.sh restart

The opers on the servers get this notice:

*** Notice -- avade_uhm: host-masking salt set by OperServ

It can also be checked on each server, as server admin:

/MODULE CMD avade_uhm

which should answer "salt set by services".

Changing the salt later changes every mask. Bans, akicks and access masks that
were set on the old masks stop matching.


10.4 Turn it on

/OperServ UHM 1 1

The first value is the type of masking: 1 is the one the module does, 0 is off.
The second value is the user mode +H:

0   users cannot use it
1   everyone gets it when they connect
2   users may set it themselves

"/OperServ UHM" shows the setting and "/OperServ UHM 0 0" turns the masking
off. The servers remember the setting.

Users that were already online keep the host they had until they reconnect.

A user who wants to show the real host can turn the mask off for themselves
with "/NickServ SET SHOWHOST ON". A vhost (SET VHOST) goes before both.


10.5 Check that it works

Services and every server must give the same mask for the same host:

/MODULE CMD avade_uhm TEST c-83-233-12-7.bredband.telia.com 83.233.12.7
/OperServ UHM TEST c-83-233-12-7.bredband.telia.com 83.233.12.7

Then connect a new client. It gets +H, and a /WHOIS on it shows the masked
host. "/OperServ UINFO <nick>" shows the same host on the line "Shown as".


10.6 If something goes wrong

"Module load error for avade_uhm: Incompatible module (My interface version:
1011 Module version: 1010)"
    The module was built from the source of another bahamut version than the
    one that runs. Build it again from the right source (10.1).

build.sh: "has module interface 1010, bahamut 2.2.0 or newer (1011) is needed"
    The same thing: that source directory is older than bahamut 2.2.0.

build.sh: "run ./configure in ... first"
    Run ./configure in the bahamut source, with the same options as when the
    ircd was built. It does not have to be compiled or installed again.

"Module load error for avade_uhm: ... cannot open shared object file"
    avade_uhm.so is not in the modules directory of the ircd. Give build.sh
    the directory of the running ircd as the second path.

"Permission Denied, You do not have the correct irc operator privileges"
    MODULE LOAD and MODULE CMD are for server admins (access A in the oper
    block), and only on the server they are connected to.

"I don't have module support."
    The ircd was built with --disable-hookmodules. Build bahamut again
    without it.

/MODULE LIST is empty after a /REHASH
    A rehash does not load modules: use /MODULE LOAD avade_uhm (10.2).

/MODULE CMD avade_uhm says "salt not set yet"
    Services have not sent the salt to that server. Check that uhmsalt is in
    services.conf, that services are linked, and that the name of services is
    in the super block of the ircd.conf.

The two TEST commands in 10.5 give different masks
    That server does not have the salt from services (see above).

"ConfigError: uhmsalt ..." or "ConfigError: uhmprefix ..." when services start
    The salt has other characters than letters and digits, is shorter than 16
    or longer than 128, or is the same as secretsalt. The prefix is 1 to 16
    letters and digits.


11. Mail

Avade mails users to confirm the mail address of a new nick, a new address or
a new password (SET EMAIL, SET PASSWD), and to send a code with which a lost
password can be replaced (/NickServ RESETPASS).

How it works:

    Avade --(insert, status 1)--> mailbox table <--(read, set status)-- AvadeMailer --> mail server

- Avade never talks to a mail server. It puts every mail in the mailbox table
  of the database and goes on, so services never wait for a slow or broken
  mail server.
- AvadeMailer (mailer.jar) is a program of its own. It looks in the mailbox
  every few seconds, sends what is waiting and sets the status. It can be
  stopped, restarted or moved to another machine without touching services,
  it only needs the database.
- The mail addresses are kept encrypted with secretsalt (table maillog). The
  mailbox has the address of each mail in clear, so the mailer does not need
  the salt, and should not have it.
- Finished mails (sent, not sent, failed) are removed from the mailbox after
  14 days: they hold addresses and codes in clear.

The status of a mail in the mailbox:

    1    waiting, put there by Avade
    2    being sent right now
    0    sent
    3    not sent, because mailer.conf has "send: false"
    500  failed: the mail server refused it (5xx), or turned it away for now
         (4xx) more times than "retries", or the mailer stopped while sending it

A mail is never sent twice by itself. A mail that was being sent when the
mailer stopped may or may not have gone out, so it is marked failed and is
only sent again with "resend".

11.1 A database user for the mailer (optional)

The mailer can use the database login of services, which is the simplest. A
user of its own that can do nothing but use the mailbox table is safer:

    grant select, update, delete on avade.mailbox to 'mailer'@'localhost' identified by 'MAILERPASS';

Use the host the mailer connects from instead of localhost when it runs on
another machine.

11.2 mailer.conf

"./avade.sh mailer start" writes mailer.conf for you the first time: it asks
for the database login (the one of services, or the user from 11.1) and the
mail server, sends a test mail to an address you give, and asks whether mails
should be sent for real. "./avade.sh mailer setup" does only that.

By hand instead:

    cd ~/avade
    cp mailer-template.conf mailer.conf
    chmod 600 mailer.conf

Set the database user from 11.1 (the mysql settings have the same names as in
services.conf), the mail server, and "send: true". Every setting is explained
in mailer-reference.conf.

With Amazon SES:
- smtphost email-smtp.<region>.amazonaws.com, smtpport 587, smtptls: true,
  smtpauth: true.
- smtpuser and smtppass are the SMTP credentials made in the SES console, not
  the AWS access keys.
- smtpfrom must be a verified address, or an address in a verified domain.
- A new SES account is in the sandbox: it only sends to verified addresses,
  one mail a second. Ask AWS for production access before going live.

With a mail server on the same machine (postfix, sendmail): smtphost
localhost, smtpport 25, smtptls: false, smtpauth: false.

11.3 Starting and checking

    ./avade.sh mailer start
    ./avade.sh mailer queue         # mails per status
    ./avade.sh mailer log           # follow the output

mailer.log has one line per mail: its id and subject, never the address or
the text. To find the address of a mail, look it up by id in the mailbox.

./avade.sh check (the cron line in 6.) also starts the mailer when it is not
running, as soon as mailer.conf exists.

11.4 Commands

    ./avade.sh mailer start|stop|restart|status|log
    ./avade.sh mailer queue                 mails in the mailbox per status
    ./avade.sh mailer resend <id>           send that mail again
    ./avade.sh mailer resend failed         send all failed mails again
    ./avade.sh mailer test <address>        send a test mail
    ./avade.sh mailer setup                 write mailer.conf by asking

11.5 A test network with a copy of real data

The addresses in a copy of a production database belong to real people. Set
"send: false": every mail is marked not sent (3) and logged, nothing leaves
the machine. Never run a mailer with "send: true" against such a copy.

11.6 Moving from an older mailer

AvadeMailer uses the same statuses as the mailers before it (1 waiting, 0
sent, 500 failed). Stop the old mailer first, then start this one: two
mailers on the same mailbox send every mail twice.

11.7 When something is wrong

"Config error: ..." when the mailer starts
    A setting is missing or wrong, the message tells which.

"No database connection: ..."
    Check the mysql settings and the grant in 11.1. It tries again by itself.

"Mail server problem, trying again later: ..."
    The mail server could not be reached, the login failed, or the certificate
    is not valid for the host (smtptls: true checks it). The mail stays waiting
    and is sent when the server works again.

"Failed: mail <id>, the server answered 554" (or another 5xx)
    The mail server refused the mail. With SES this is often a sender that is
    not verified, or a recipient that is not verified while in the sandbox.

Mails stay at status 1
    The mailer is not running: ./avade.sh mailer status


############
### HELP ###
############

If you followed these instructions and did not manage to get a fully working
services on your bahamut based network. You may join #avade in irc.avade.net
for for further help.

